\ Target cell width in bytes: 8 by default (x86-64, ARM64, ...). To
\ cross-compile for a 32-bit target (e.g. i386) instead, edit the "8"
\ below to "4" (see README.md) - this is deliberately a plain, direct
\ edit rather than something conditional/settable-before-including: an
\ earlier attempt at that used IF/THEN at the top level (interpret
\ mode) to skip re-creating the variable if the person had already set
\ one, but IF/THEN here are compile-only words - they only work inside
\ a :...; definition, where the compiler processes tokens up front. At
\ the top level they silently corrupt the dictionary instead of
\ erroring (found via PROGRESS.md's account of a long debugging session
\ - see it before reintroducing anything like this). Everything
\ cell-width-dependent in this file and in kernel.4 derives from this
\ one place.
VARIABLE TARGET-CELL-BYTES
8 TARGET-CELL-BYTES !

\ PART 1: THE VOCABULARIES.

VOCABULARY TARGET
\ This vocabulary will hold shadow definitions for all words that are in
\ the target dictionary. When a shadow definition is executed, it
\ performs the compile action in the target dictionary.

VOCABULARY TRANSIENT
\ This vocabulary will hold definitions that must be executed by the
\ host system ( the system on which the cross compiler runs) and that
\ compile to the target system.

\ Expl: The word IF occurs in all three vocabularies. The word IF in the
\       FORTH vocabulary is run by the host system and is used when
\       compiling host definitions. A different version is in the
\       TRANSIENT vocabulary. This one runs on the host system and
\       is used when compiling target definitions. The version in the
\       TARGET vocabulary is the version that will run on the target
\       system.


\ PART 2: THE TARGET DICTIONARY SPACE.

\ Next we need to define the target space and the words to access it.

40000 CONSTANT IMAGE_SIZE

CREATE IMAGE IMAGE_SIZE CHARS ALLOT \ This space contains the target image.
       IMAGE IMAGE_SIZE 0 FILL      \ Initialize it to zero.

\ Fetch and store characters in the target space.
: C@-T ( t-addr --- c) CHARS IMAGE + C@ ;
: C!-T ( c t-addr ---) CHARS IMAGE + C! ;

\ Fetch and store cells in the target space, TARGET-CELL-BYTES wide.
\ As of phase 5 (see GOALS.md), images are native host endianness, not a
\ portable on-disk format. These build/take apart a little-endian value
\ byte by byte via C@-T/C!-T (not plain @/! on this host), because
\ that's what makes the *disk file* end up in the intended byte order:
\ WRITE-FILE (on whatever engine happens to be running this) reverses
\ whatever it writes, and C@-T/C!-T's own byte-level access already
\ cancels that reversal out (see PROGRESS.md for the derivation) - the
\ same reason name strings/PLACE-T never needed any special handling
\ either. Safe on this host regardless of TARGET-CELL-BYTES: this host's
\ own cells are always at least as wide as the target's (see PROGRESS.md,
\ phase 2, for what goes wrong when that's not true, and why building a
\ *narrower* target from a wider host - unlike phase 2's situation - has
\ no equivalent problem).
\ BEGIN/WHILE/REPEAT with an explicit counter, not DO/LOOP: DO/LOOP here
\ reliably hung the bootstrap somewhere past kernel.4 loading (found by
\ bisection, not fully root-caused - see PROGRESS.md). These are called
\ often enough, from enough different contexts, that avoiding DO/LOOP's
\ own return-stack usage entirely was the safer fix.
: @-T  ( t-addr --- x)
  0 0
  BEGIN
    DUP TARGET-CELL-BYTES @ <
  WHILE
    >R OVER R@ CHARS + C@-T R@ 8 * LSHIFT OR R> 1+
  REPEAT
  DROP SWAP DROP
  \ Sign-extend when the target cell is narrower than this host's own
  \ (this host's cells are always at least as wide as the target's, see
  \ the note on C@-T/C!-T above): the loop above only fills the low
  \ TARGET-CELL-BYTES bytes, leaving the high bytes zero regardless of
  \ the target value's actual sign. Negative target-level sentinels
  \ (FORWARD's own -1, used by RESOLVE's chain-termination check) need
  \ those high bytes properly extended to compare correctly against
  \ host -1 - otherwise RESOLVE's BEGIN/WHILE loop never sees them as
  \ equal and walks off the end of the chain into unrelated memory.
  TARGET-CELL-BYTES @ 8 < IF
    DUP 1 TARGET-CELL-BYTES @ 8 * 1 - LSHIFT AND IF
      -1 TARGET-CELL-BYTES @ 8 * LSHIFT OR
    THEN
  THEN ;
: !-T  ( x t-addr ---)
  0
  BEGIN
    DUP TARGET-CELL-BYTES @ <
  WHILE
    >R OVER R@ 8 * RSHIFT OVER R@ CHARS + C!-T R> 1+
  REPEAT
  DROP 2DROP ;

\ A dictionary is constructed in the target space. Here are the primitives
\ to maintain the dictionary pointer and to reserve space.

VARIABLE DP-T                       \ Dictionary pointer for target dictionary.
0 DP-T !                            \ Initialize it to zero, SOD starts at 0.
: THERE ( --- t-addr) DP-T @ ;      \ Equivalent of HERE in target space.
: ALLOT-T ( n --- ) DP-T +! ;       \ Reserve n bytes in the dictionary.
: CHARS-T ( n1 --- n2 ) ;
: CELLS-T ( n1 --- n2 ) TARGET-CELL-BYTES @ * ;  \ n1 target cells in bytes.
: ALIGN-T                           \ Target only accesses cells at aligned
                                    \ addresses.
  BEGIN THERE TARGET-CELL-BYTES @ 1- AND WHILE 1 ALLOT-T REPEAT ;
: ALIGNED-T ( n1 --- n2 )
  TARGET-CELL-BYTES @ 1- + TARGET-CELL-BYTES @ NEGATE AND ;
: C,-T  ( c --- )  THERE C!-T 1 CHARS ALLOT-T ;
: ,-T   ( x --- )  THERE !-T  1 CELLS-T ALLOT-T ;

: PLACE-T ( c-addr len t-addr --- ) \ Move counted string to target space.
  OVER OVER C!-T 1+ CHARS IMAGE + SWAP CHARS CMOVE ;

\ 8-byte magic written before the image itself, so a future engine with
\ a different cell width refuses a mismatched image instead of silently
\ misbehaving. Plain byte-level writes - byte order doesn't apply to a
\ byte sequence, so this needs no special handling (same reasoning as
\ @-T/!-T above). Plain C! (not C!-T): IMAGE-MAGIC is its own buffer,
\ not part of IMAGE.
CREATE IMAGE-MAGIC 8 CHARS ALLOT
: INIT-IMAGE-MAGIC
  82 IMAGE-MAGIC C! 69 IMAGE-MAGIC 1 CHARS + C!
  76 IMAGE-MAGIC 2 CHARS + C! 70 IMAGE-MAGIC 3 CHARS + C! \ "RELF"
  TARGET-CELL-BYTES @ IMAGE-MAGIC 4 CHARS + C!             \ cell width
  0 IMAGE-MAGIC 5 CHARS + C!
  0 IMAGE-MAGIC 6 CHARS + C!
  0 IMAGE-MAGIC 7 CHARS + C! ;
INIT-IMAGE-MAGIC

\ After the Forth system is constructed, its image must be saved.
: SAVE-IMAGE ( "name" --- )
  32 WORD COUNT W/O BIN CREATE-FILE ABORT" Can't create file" >R
  IMAGE-MAGIC 8 R@ WRITE-FILE ABORT" Can't write file"
  IMAGE THERE R@ WRITE-FILE ABORT" Can't write file"
  R> CLOSE-FILE ABORT" Can't close file" ;

\ PART 3: CREATING NEW DEFINITIONS IN THE TARGET SYSTEM.

\ These words create new target definitions, both the shadow definition
\ and the header in the target dictionary. The layout of target headers
\ can be changed but FIND in the target system must be changed accordingly.

VARIABLE TLINK 0 TLINK !   \ This variable points to the name
                           \ of the last definition.

: "HEADER >IN @ CREATE >IN ! \ Create the shadow definition.
  ALIGN-T
  TLINK @ IF
    TLINK @ THERE - ,-T        \ Lay out the link field.
  ELSE
    0 ,-T
  THEN
  BL WORD
  COUNT DUP >R THERE PLACE-T  \ Place name in target dictionary.
  THERE TLINK !
  THERE C@-T 128 OR THERE C!-T R> 1+ ALLOT-T ALIGN-T ;
      \ Set bit 7 of count byte as a marker.

\ : "HEADER CREATE ALIGN-T ;  \ Alternative for "HEADER in case the target system
                      \ is just an application without headers.

ALSO TRANSIENT DEFINITIONS

: IMMEDIATE TLINK @ DUP C@-T 64 OR SWAP C!-T ;
            \ Set the IMMEDIATE bit of last name.

PREVIOUS DEFINITIONS


\ PART 4: CODE GENERATION

VARIABLE STATE-T 0 STATE-T ! \ State variable for cross compiler.
: T] 1 STATE-T ! ;
: T[ 0 STATE-T ! ;

VARIABLE CSP   \ Stack pointer checking between : and ;
: !CSP DEPTH CSP ! ;
: ?CSP DEPTH CSP @ - ABORT" Incomplete control structure" ;

VARIABLE LAST-PRIMITIVE 1 LAST-PRIMITIVE !

\ Primitive-dispatch tokens (and a couple of other cell-width-dependent
\ values) as formulas in TARGET-CELL-BYTES, instead of the hardcoded
\ numbers phase 2 used (9/5/17/13/etc for the then-only stride-8
\ numbering). token = 1 + (position-1)*stride, where position is the
\ primitive's 1-based position in kernel.4's PRIMITIVE list (see
\ PROGRESS.md, 2026-09-01, for what goes wrong if these are hardcoded
\ instead and the stride or list ever changes) and stride ==
\ TARGET-CELL-BYTES (dispatch-table entries are pointer-sized, which is
\ exactly the cell width on every host this targets - see relf.c).
: EXIT-TOKEN    ( --- n ) 1 TARGET-CELL-BYTES @ + ;         \ 2nd: EXIT
: LIT-TOKEN     ( --- n ) 1 2 TARGET-CELL-BYTES @ * + ;     \ 3rd: LIT
: BRANCH-TOKEN  ( --- n ) 1 3 TARGET-CELL-BYTES @ * + ;     \ 4th: BRANCH
: 0BRANCH-TOKEN ( --- n ) 1 4 TARGET-CELL-BYTES @ * + ;     \ 5th: 0BRANCH
: RFROM-TOKEN   ( --- n ) 1 17 TARGET-CELL-BYTES @ * + ;    \ 18th: R>
\ Not primitive tokens, but the same "must vary with TARGET-CELL-BYTES,
\ and gets embedded as a literal into a *different* word's compiled
\ body, so a plain host IF/ELSE can't fix it" problem: kernel.4's
\ CELL+/CELLS/CELL-/DEPTH/ALIGNED/ALIGN/J, and 2/'s sign-bit shift.
: CELLBYTES-TOKEN  ( --- n ) TARGET-CELL-BYTES @ ;
: CELLSHIFT-TOKEN  ( --- n ) TARGET-CELL-BYTES @ 4 = IF 2 ELSE 3 THEN ;
: SIGNSHIFT-TOKEN  ( --- n ) TARGET-CELL-BYTES @ 8 * 1- ;

\ Primitive tokens below are updated for the stride-8 numbering (see
\ PRIMITIVE above): EXIT=9, LIT=17, BRANCH=25, 0BRANCH=33. Same values
\ as the formulas above at TARGET-CELL-BYTES=8; the formulas replace
\ the phase-2 hardcoded numbers so a 4-byte-cell target gets the right
\ ones automatically instead of needing every occurrence hand-updated.
: LITERAL-T ( n --- ) LIT-TOKEN ,-T ,-T ;

ONLY FORTH ALSO TRANSIENT DEFINITIONS FORTH
\ Now define the words that do compile code.

: PRIMITIVE ( c --- )
  \ Primitive tokens stride by sizeof(host function pointer), which is
  \ TARGET-CELL-BYTES on every host this targets, see GOALS.md.
  LAST-PRIMITIVE @ DUP TARGET-CELL-BYTES @ LAST-PRIMITIVE +!
  "HEADER DUP , ,-T EXIT-TOKEN ,-T  \ Create an executable target definition.
  TLINK @ DUP C@-T 32 OR SWAP C!-T   \ Set the MACRO bit of last name. 
  DOES> @ ,-T
;

\ TRANSIENT-vocabulary wrappers around the -TOKEN words above, for use
\ *inside* kernel.4's own target-level colon-definitions (CONSTANT,
\ LITERAL, ;, BEGIN/UNTIL/IF/ELSE/WHILE/REPEAT, (;CODE)'s R> token,
\ CELL+/CELLS/CELL-/DEPTH/ALIGNED/ALIGN/J, 2/'s sign mask). A bare
\ number there gets compiled as a literal into whatever *target*
\ definition kernel.4 is building at the time (via CROSS-COMPILE's
\ NUMBER?/LITERAL-T path - see PROGRESS.md for the full derivation of
\ why this needs its own mechanism rather than an ordinary host IF/
\ ELSE); these words, found via CROSS-COMPILE's restricted TARGET+
\ TRANSIENT search and executed the moment kernel.4 reaches them,
\ reproduce exactly that compiled-literal effect with the right value.
: EXIT-TOK    EXIT-TOKEN LITERAL-T ;
: LIT-TOK     LIT-TOKEN LITERAL-T ;
: BRANCH-TOK  BRANCH-TOKEN LITERAL-T ;
: 0BRANCH-TOK 0BRANCH-TOKEN LITERAL-T ;
: RFROM-TOK   RFROM-TOKEN LITERAL-T ;
: CELLBYTES-TOK CELLBYTES-TOKEN LITERAL-T ;
: CELLSHIFT-TOK CELLSHIFT-TOKEN LITERAL-T ;
: SIGNSHIFT-TOK SIGNSHIFT-TOKEN LITERAL-T ;
FORTH DEFINITIONS

\ PART 5: FORWARD REFERENCES

\ Some definitions are referenced before they are defined. A definition
\ in the TRANSIENT voc is created for each forward referenced definition.
\ This links all addresses together where the forward reference is used.
\ The word RESOLVE stores the real address everywhere it is needed.
\
\ FORWARD and RESOLVE are defined here, before :/; below get redefined -
\ deliberately, not incidentally. They're ordinary host-level defining
\ words (like VARIABLE would be at the host level), not target-shadow
\ words like PRIMITIVE/CONSTANT/VARIABLE further down, so they don't
\ need :/;'s "HEADER-based target-compilation machinery - and they
\ actively can't tolerate it: that redefined : never sets the base
\ kernel's own STATE flag (only its own separate STATE-T), so an
\ ordinary CREATE/DOES>-based word compiled under it never actually
\ gets compiled - it executes immediately, at definition time, with
\ whatever happens to be on the stack at that moment instead of the
\ arguments it's meant to receive when properly invoked later. That's a
\ silent, accidental "works" for definitions robust to it, and a crash
\ for FORWARD specifically (see PROGRESS.md for the full diagnosis, found
\ via gdb dictionary-chain walking and primitive-level trace-diffing).
\ Defining FORWARD/RESOLVE here, before the redefinition, sidesteps the
\ whole issue rather than depending on it.

: FORWARD
  CREATE -1 ,              \ Store head of list in the definition.
  DOES> 
        DUP @ ,-T THERE 1 CELLS-T - SWAP ! \ Reserve a cell in the dictionary
                  \ where the call to the forward definition must come.
                  \ As the call address is unknown, store link to next
                  \ reference instead.
;

: RESOLVE
  ALSO TARGET >IN @ ' >BODY @ >R >IN ! \ Find the resolving word in the
                          \ target voc. and take the CFA out of the definition.
  TRANSIENT ' >BODY  @                 \ Find the forward ref word in the
                                       \ TRANSIENT VOC and take list head.
  BEGIN
   DUP -1 -                            \ Traverse all the links until end.
  WHILE
   DUP @-T                             \ Take address of next link from dict.
   R@ ROT SWAP OVER - CELLBYTES-TOKEN - SWAP !-T   \ Set resolved address in dict.
  REPEAT DROP R> DROP PREVIOUS
;

\ T' and >BODY-T are, like FORWARD/RESOLVE above, ordinary host-level
\ utility words with no need for :/;'s target-shadow machinery - and,
\ also like FORWARD/RESOLVE, they can't tolerate being defined under
\ it either (see the note above FORWARD for why). POSTPONE and ['] later
\ reference T' from within their own (correctly target-shadowed) bodies;
\ that's fine regardless of where T' itself was defined, as long as
\ it's defined before they are - which it is, here.

: T' ( --- t-addr) \ Find the execution token of a target definition.
  ALSO TARGET ' >BODY @ \ Get the address from the shadow definition.
  PREVIOUS
;

: >BODY-T ( t-addr1 --- t-addr2 ) \ Convert executing token to param address.
  \ NOTE: this is target-address arithmetic and must use CELLS-T, not the
  \ host's CELLS. It only worked before because the host (RelF, 4-byte
  \ cells) and the target (RelF, 4-byte cells) happened to match; see
  \ GOALS.md for the 8-byte target/host mismatch this now avoids.
  1 CELLS-T + ;

\ PART 6: DEFINING WORDS.

TRANSIENT DEFINITIONS FORTH

FORWARD DOVAR \ Dovar is the runtime part of a variable.

\ VARIABLE and CONSTANT don't need :/;'s inherited DOES> either - they
\ each have their own complete "HEADER...DOES> structure right in their
\ own body text (same as FORWARD/RESOLVE/T'/>BODY-T above), and, for the
\ same reason, can't tolerate the nested-DOES> conflict the redefined :
\ below would cause. Defined here with the plain :/; instead, so their
\ body actually compiles instead of executing immediately. T]/T[ are
\ applied explicitly around each so STATE-T is still set correctly -
\ NUMBER? needs that to compile their own numeric literals (VARIABLE's
\ "0", CONSTANT's "1") as target literals via LITERAL-T, same as the
\ redefined : below would have done, without what broke it.

T]
: VARIABLE "HEADER THERE , [ TRANSIENT ] DOVAR [ FORTH ]  0 ,-T
\ Create a variable.
  DOES> @ THERE - CELLBYTES-TOKEN - ,-T ;
T[

T]
: CONSTANT "HEADER THERE ,
  LIT-TOKEN ,-T ,-T EXIT-TOKEN ,-T \ Assemble the instruction LIT with EXIT.
  DOES> @ 1 CELLS-T + @-T LITERAL-T \ Compile const as a literal for speed.
;
T[

FORTH DEFINITIONS

\ PART 5b: TARGET-LEVEL COLON-DEFINITION SHADOW WORDS.
\
\ The control-structure words below (PART 7), similarly, are plain
\ words with no nested defining-word pattern of their own - so, same as
\ FORWARD/RESOLVE/T'/>BODY-T/VARIABLE/CONSTANT above, they're defined
\ with the plain :/; too (wrapped in one T]...T[ pair for the whole
\ block, since nothing here needs :/;'s "HEADER-inherited DOES> either -
\ nothing here turned out to actually need the redefined :/; at all).

FORTH DEFINITIONS

\ PART 7: COMPILING WORDS

TRANSIENT DEFINITIONS FORTH

T]

\ NOTE: these reserve a target cell, so they must use CELLS-T, not the
\ host's CELLS (see >BODY-T above for why this distinction now matters).
: BEGIN THERE ;
: UNTIL 0BRANCH-TOKEN ,-T THERE - ,-T ;
: IF 0BRANCH-TOKEN ,-T THERE 1 CELLS-T ALLOT-T ;
: THEN THERE OVER - SWAP !-T ;
: ELSE BRANCH-TOKEN ,-T THERE 1 CELLS-T ALLOT-T SWAP THERE OVER - SWAP !-T ;
: WHILE 0BRANCH-TOKEN ,-T THERE 1 CELLS-T ALLOT-T SWAP ;
: REPEAT BRANCH-TOKEN ,-T THERE - ,-T THERE OVER - SWAP !-T ;

FORWARD (DO)
FORWARD (LOOP)
FORWARD (.")
FORWARD (ABORT")
FORWARD (POSTPONE)

: DO [ TRANSIENT ] (DO) [ FORTH ] THERE ;
: LOOP [ TRANSIENT ] (LOOP) [ FORTH ] THERE - ,-T ;
: ." [ TRANSIENT ] (.") [ FORTH ] 34 WORD COUNT DUP 1+ >R
      THERE PLACE-T R> ALLOT-T ALIGN-T ;
: POSTPONE [ TRANSIENT ] (POSTPONE) [ FORTH ] T' THERE - ,-T ;
: ABORT" [ TRANSIENT ] (ABORT") [ FORTH ] 34 WORD COUNT DUP 1+ >R
      THERE PLACE-T R> ALLOT-T ALIGN-T ;

: \ POSTPONE \ ; IMMEDIATE
: ( POSTPONE ( ; IMMEDIATE \ Move duplicates of comment words to TRANSIENT
: CHARS-T CHARS-T ; \ Also words that must be executed while cross compiling.
: CELLS-T CELLS-T ;
: ALLOT-T ALLOT-T ;
: ['] T' LITERAL-T ;

T[

FORTH DEFINITIONS

\ kernel.4 itself uses plain :/; throughout its own source to compile
\ *target*-level colon definitions - CROSS-COMPILE finds those via its
\ own restricted [TARGET, TRANSIENT] search, so a :/; pair needs to
\ exist in TRANSIENT vocabulary for it to find. This is the redefined,
\ "HEADER-based one - safe here because kernel.4's own colon-definitions
\ don't have the nested-DOES> pattern that broke it for FORWARD/VARIABLE/
\ the control-structure words above (none of which use it anymore).

TRANSIENT DEFINITIONS FORTH

: : !CSP "HEADER THERE , T] DOES> @ THERE - CELLBYTES-TOKEN - ,-T ;

: ; EXIT-TOKEN ,-T T[ ?CSP ; \ Compile EXIT. Quit compilation state.

FORTH DEFINITIONS

\ PART 8: THE CROSS COMPILER ITSELF.

VARIABLE DPL
: NUMBER? ( c-addr ---- d f)
  -1 DPL !
  BASE @ >R
  COUNT
  OVER C@ 45 = DUP >R IF 1 - SWAP 1 + SWAP THEN \ Get any - sign
  OVER C@ 36 = IF 16 BASE ! 1 - SWAP 1 + SWAP THEN   \ $ sign for hex.
  OVER C@ 35 = IF 10 BASE ! 1 - SWAP 1 + SWAP THEN   \ # sign for decimal
  DUP  0 > 0= IF  R> DROP R> BASE ! 0 EXIT THEN   \ Length 0 or less?
  >R >R 0 0 R> R>
  BEGIN
   >NUMBER
   DUP IF OVER C@ 46 = IF 1 - DUP DPL ! SWAP 1 + SWAP ELSE \ handle point.
         R> DROP R> BASE ! 0 EXIT THEN   \ Error if anything but point
       THEN
  DUP 0= UNTIL DROP DROP R> IF DNEGATE THEN
  R> BASE ! -1
;


: CROSS-COMPILE
  ONLY TARGET DEFINITIONS ALSO TRANSIENT \ Restrict search order.
  BEGIN
   BL WORD
   DUP C@ 0= IF \ Get new word
    DROP REFILL DROP                      \ If empty, get new line.
   ELSE
    DUP COUNT S" END-CROSS" COMPARE 0=    \ Exit cross compiler on END-CROSS
    IF
     ONLY FORTH ALSO DEFINITIONS          \ Normal search order again.
     DROP EXIT
    THEN
    FIND IF                               \ Execute if found.
     EXECUTE
    ELSE
     NUMBER? 0= ABORT" Undefined word" DROP
     STATE-T @ IF \ Parse it as a number.
      LITERAL-T   \ If compiling then compile as a literal.
     THEN
    THEN
   THEN
  0 UNTIL
;

\ PART 9: CROSS COMPILING THE KERNEL

\ Up till now not a single byte of the new Forth kernel has actually been
\ compiled.

TRANSIENT DEFINITIONS
FORWARD COLD
FORWARD WARM
FORWARD DIV-EX
FORWARD BREAK-EX
FORWARD TIMER-EX
FORWARD THROW
FORTH DEFINITIONS

S" kernel.4" INCLUDED

\ PART 10: FINISHING AND SAVING THE TARGET IMAGE.

\ Resolve the forward references created by the cross compiler.
RESOLVE (DO)
RESOLVE DOVAR
RESOLVE (LOOP)
RESOLVE (.")
RESOLVE (ABORT")
RESOLVE COLD
RESOLVE WARM
\  RESOLVE DIV-EX
\  RESOLVE BREAK-EX
\  RESOLVE TIMER-EX
RESOLVE THROW
RESOLVE (POSTPONE)

\ Store appropriate values into some of the new Forth's variables.
TLINK @ T' FORTH-WORDLIST >BODY-T !-T
THERE   T' DP             >BODY-T !-T

SAVE-IMAGE kernel.img \ Save the newly constructed Forth system to disk.

BYE
